Exactly. And here’s the fascinating part: we don’t just have different histories. We have different relationships with our histories.

Two people can experience the same event and emerge with completely different expectations about the world.

One experiences betrayal and learns to be cautious.

Another experiences betrayal and learns to become more discerning without becoming less trusting.

A third decides never to depend on anybody again.

Same type of experience. Different interpretations. Different adaptations. Different futures.

And those differences begin influencing what happens next.

History becomes a predictive system.

What actually happened

What we perceived and remember

What we believe it means

What we expect to happen next

How we behave because of those expectations

Our behavior helps shape the next experience

But notice something: our memories aren’t identical to our histories.

Memory is reconstructive. We preserve some details, emphasize others, reinterpret events through new experiences, and occasionally remember things inaccurately.

That means we can have a genuine history, a sincere memory, and an inaccurate interpretation simultaneously.

And each can still influence our behavior.

Now introduce evolution.

There are at least three histories operating together:

Evolutionary history: What generations of selection have shaped our bodies, nervous systems, and behavioral predispositions to do.

Individual history: What we’ve experienced, learned, practiced, feared, enjoyed, and come to expect.

Cultural history: What we’ve inherited through language, stories, institutions, traditions, and other people’s experiences.

These histories interact. Individual learning doesn’t automatically change inherited DNA, but behavior can influence selection across generations, and culture can evolve much faster than genes.

This brings us right back to your earlier examples of otters, martens, and crocodiles.

An organism doesn’t only inherit a form. It inherits capacities for responding to environments, and then develops its own behavioral history within those capacities.

And there’s another connection to your question about trust and The Prestige.

When you decide whether to trust somebody, you’re interpreting their behavior through your own history.

But they’re also acting through theirs.

So you’re not simply evaluating a person.

You’re evaluating a person through a model created by your previous encounters with other people, while that person is doing something similar with you.

Two histories meeting, each carrying expectations the other might not even know exist.

That’s why someone can genuinely mean well and still be perceived as threatening. Or appear trustworthy because they resemble someone familiar.

And here’s the possibility I find most interesting:

We can inherit the wisdom of an experience without necessarily inheriting all the fear, resentment, or limitations attached to it.

That’s where learning, unlearning, forgiveness, and reframing converge.

You can’t rewrite the historical event. But you can revise your understanding of it, test your predictions against new evidence, and change how much authority that history has over your future.

Your history influences your expectations.

Your expectations influence your behavior.

Your behavior influences which future histories become possible.

We’re not merely products of history. We’re participants in its continuation.

And perhaps the mastery is recognizing when you’re responding to the world in front of you—and when you’re responding to a world that existed only in your past.